<!--Post: 4436--><!--Time: 1106361272-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4436"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_kelleher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_kelleher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 21, 2005 - 6:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->These are additional observations that I would like to add to my original profile on Tom’s website: <BR> <BR>- BTK was probably born between 1946 and 1952, with a higher probability of 1949-1951. He is a baby-boomer. <BR> <BR>- He was probably the youngest child with at least one older, female sibling. There were possibly multiple older siblings. At least one older, female sibling proved quite successful and was recognized both inside and outside the family home as “special.” <BR> <BR>- His mother was probably passive, perhaps well educated, a reader, and a traditional housewife/mother. She “married below her station” in the traditional sense. She may have been religious and conservative. <BR> <BR>-His father &#40;if present&#41; was probably less educated than his mother, dominant, possibly physical and threatening, and was probably employed in the trades or a job that required significant physical labor. His relationship with BTK would not have been intimate. There is no evidence of early childhood abuse. <BR> <BR>- The family home would have been comfortable but not extravagant. Children were urged to earn their allowances and earn their own money. <BR> <BR>- BTK probably suffered either a significant loss or a significant event of rejection around the age of 20-21. This may have either involved his mother or a love relationship that was somehow destroyed. This trauma is integral to his crimes. <BR> <BR>- BTK is a reader but not a particularly avid one. Among his favorite subjects would be true crime and practical aberrant psychology. He would rather read a short article than a book, rather watch a TV documentary than do any serious research. <BR> <BR>- He probably plays a stringed instrument, like a guitar, but not particularly well. This would have been of interest to him in his twenties but probably not long after that. <BR> <BR>- He probably has a decent collection of movies, either taped or on DVD. These would likely be drama or crime-related. <BR> <BR>- He probably has a decent collection of music that spans several decades. He is aware of music trends but not obsessed with them. He prefers simple, straight-forward musical genres. <BR> <BR>- He has a good memory and can recall facts from many years ago. Some may say that his memory is exceptional. <BR> <BR>- Like his mother, he is also passive and a manipulator, but very good at presenting himself to others even though he shies away from social interaction for the most part. <BR> <BR>- He probably entered into a long-term relationship in his late twenties or early thirties. His wife would be much like his mother &#40;passive, unquestioning, and more attached to the home than places outside the home&#41;. She may have died or left the relationship in recent years. If not, she has withdrawn from BTK over the years, to the extent that they now have virtually no meaningful communication and may not have had any for a long time. <BR> <BR>- He was worked at jobs that are below his intelligence level, which is above average. He would always take a safe course at work and do his best to be admired by his superiors, much as he worked at being a “parent pleaser” in his youth. <BR> <BR>- He probably did not change jobs often after his early thirties and has been viewed as a diligent but uninspired employee. <BR> <BR>- He is a true sociopath, incapable of identifying with others at virtually any level. He enjoyed killing but was always very careful about controlling as much of the event as possible. <BR> <BR>- He sees himself as a poet but has not read much poetry. This vision of himself came from encounters in his early college years &#40;see the first part of the profile on this point&#41;. <BR> <BR>- He is not particularly strong in a physical sense but can convey strength through his speech. He is likely to wear bulky clothing during his crimes.  <BR> <BR>- In his younger days, BTK was a passive stalker, regularly stalking potential victims but not acting out on his fantasies. He is unlikely to have an arrest record &#40;or one of any consequence&#41;. <BR> <BR>- He has a very poor opinion of women, much like his father’s opinion. He may voice a more enlightened point of view but he is actually living out his father’s “silent” direction about the worth of women. This would slip through in his casual speech patterns and attempted jokes. <BR> <BR>- His physical health has deteriorated in recent years, possibly to a dangerous level. <BR> <BR>- He would be very uncomfortable with physicians, dentists, psychologists, and other health providers, probably denigrating their functions and compensating with his own form of self-healing. This may rise to the level of a phobia. He would appease other necessary authority figures to suit his needs and probably does this very effectively. <BR> <BR>- Despite his protestations to the contrary, BTK is a very controlled person, able to pass himself off convincingly in delicate situations. He would be viewed as harmless and perhaps even “easy-going” in casual relationships. However, he would be dominant and controlling in intimate relationships. <BR> <BR>- He would spend his life on the periphery of social engagements, perhaps lurking but rarely interacting with any enthusiasm. He would claim that he can’t dance, doesn’t like to drink, and would prefer a “quiet night at home” to any other activity.  <BR> <BR>- He would have few, if any, friends and would have been virtually friendless his entire life. His social base, limited as it is, was probably generated through his employment rather than any leisure activities. <BR> <BR>- His relationship with any surviving siblings would be strained, distant, or perhaps non-existent. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
